---
title: Video & timestamps
description: A player that costs nothing until it is pressed, and a list of timestamps that drives it — press a chapter to jump there; the one playing is marked as the video runs. The video counterpart of the table of contents.
eyebrow: Media
source: src/components/video.css · src/js/im-media.js
---
<h2>Player and chapters</h2>
<p>Press a timestamp — the player starts at that point. Press another while it plays and it seeks.</p>
{{#example}}
<div class="im-video-layout">
	<div class="im-video" id="demo-video" data-im-video="aqz-KE-bpKQ" data-im-video-title="Big Buck Bunny">
		<img class="im-video-poster" src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/aqz-KE-bpKQ/maxresdefault.jpg" alt="">
		<button class="im-video-play" type="button" aria-label="Play video">{{> "icons/play"}}</button>
		<div class="im-video-meta"><span>Big Buck Bunny</span><small>10:34</small></div>
	</div>
	<ol class="im-chapters" data-im-chapters="#demo-video" data-im-duration="10:34" aria-label="Chapters">
		<li><button type="button" data-t="0:00"><time>0:00</time><span>Opening titles</span><small>0:32</small></button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="0:32"><time>0:32</time><span>The meadow</span><small>1:16</small></button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="1:48"><time>1:48</time><span>Three troublemakers</span><small>2:07</small></button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="3:55"><time>3:55</time><span>The plan</span><small>2:35</small></button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="6:30"><time>6:30</time><span>Revenge</span><small>2:45</small></button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="9:15"><time>9:15</time><span>Credits</span><small>1:19</small></button></li>
	</ol>
</div>
{{/example}}

<h2>How it behaves</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Nothing loads from YouTube until play is pressed.</strong> Before that it is a picture and a button: no iframe, no cookies, no 500 KB of player. Then it comes from the no-cookie domain.</li>
	<li><strong>A chapter seeks the player</strong> — and starts it at that point if it has not been started. The player is scrolled back into view if you had scrolled past it.</li>
	<li><strong>The playing chapter is marked</strong> <code>aria-current="true"</code>, with a bar on its leading edge and its own progress drawn under it. The script talks to the player with <code>postMessage</code> — YouTube's own API script is never loaded.</li>
	<li><strong>The poster is the hit area.</strong> The round button is what you see; the whole picture is what you can press.</li>
</ul>

<h2>As chips</h2>
<p><code>im-chapters-chips</code>: the same timestamps as a wrapping row — for under a video in a blog post, where a column would be too much.</p>
{{#example}}
<div class="im-stack" style="--im-stack-gap: .875rem">
	<div class="im-video" id="demo-video-2" data-im-video="aqz-KE-bpKQ" style="max-width: 34rem">
		<img class="im-video-poster" src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/aqz-KE-bpKQ/hqdefault.jpg" alt="">
		<button class="im-video-play" type="button" aria-label="Play video">{{> "icons/play"}}</button>
	</div>
	<ol class="im-chapters im-chapters-chips" data-im-chapters="#demo-video-2" aria-label="Jump to">
		<li><button type="button" data-t="0:00"><time>0:00</time>Intro</button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="1:48"><time>1:48</time>Troublemakers</button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="3:55"><time>3:55</time>The plan</button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="6:30"><time>6:30</time>Revenge</button></li>
	</ol>
</div>
{{/example}}

<h2>A self-hosted file</h2>
<p>Put a <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> inside <code>im-video</code> and leave <code>data-im-video</code> empty. Chapters drive it the same way.</p>
{{#example}}
<div class="im-stack" style="--im-stack-gap: .875rem; max-width: 34rem">
	<div class="im-video" id="demo-video-3" data-im-video="">
		<video src="{{demo.video}}" preload="metadata" loop playsinline></video>
		<img class="im-video-poster" src="{{demo.post.feature_image}}" alt="">
		<button class="im-video-play" type="button" aria-label="Play video">{{> "icons/play"}}</button>
	</div>
	<ol class="im-chapters im-chapters-chips" data-im-chapters="#demo-video-3" aria-label="Jump to">
		<li><button type="button" data-t="0:00"><time>0:00</time>Start</button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="0:04"><time>0:04</time>Middle</button></li>
		<li><button type="button" data-t="0:08"><time>0:08</time>End</button></li>
	</ol>
</div>
{{/example}}

<h2>Markup</h2>
{{{{code "hbs"}}}}
<div class="im-video" id="talk" data-im-video="YOUTUBE_ID" data-im-video-title="{{title}}">
	<img class="im-video-poster" src="{{img_url feature_image size="l"}}" alt="">
	<button class="im-video-play" type="button" aria-label="{{t "Play video"}}">{{> "icons/play"}}</button>
</div>

<ol class="im-chapters" data-im-chapters="#talk" data-im-duration="24:10" aria-label="{{t "Chapters"}}">
	<li><button type="button" data-t="0:00"><time>0:00</time><span>Introduction</span></button></li>
	<li><button type="button" data-t="2:15"><time>2:15</time><span>The problem</span></button></li>
</ol>
{{{{/code}}}}
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th></th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>data-im-video="ID"</code></td><td>The YouTube id. Empty for a <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> inside.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>data-im-chapters="#id"</code></td><td>Which player the list drives.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>data-t="m:ss"</code></td><td>On each chapter button. <code>h:mm:ss</code> works too.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>data-im-duration="m:ss"</code></td><td>The video's length, so the <em>last</em> chapter's progress can be drawn.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>--im-video-ratio</code></td><td>16 / 9. Use 9 / 16 for a vertical video.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>In a Ghost post, authors can keep writing timestamps as a plain list — <code>2:15 The problem</code> — and a few lines of theme script can turn any list whose items start with a time into this component.</p>

<h2>Full width</h2>
<p>A player that runs the whole width of its column, with the system's own frame round it — a hairline, the page's radius, the float shadow — so it sits <em>on</em> the page rather than in a hole cut out of it. The ratio is the knob, because a 21:9 trailer and a 9:16 clip are the same component.</p>
{{#example flush=true}}
<div class="im-video im-video-full im-video-wide" data-im-video="aqz-KE-bpKQ" data-im-video-title="A wide clip">
	<img class="im-video-poster" src="{{demo.six.[2].feature_image}}" alt="" loading="lazy">
	<button class="im-video-play" type="button" aria-label="Play">{{> "icons/play"}}</button>
	<div class="im-video-meta"><strong>The whole road, in eight minutes</strong><small>8:12</small></div>
</div>
{{/example}}
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th></th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>im-video-full</code></td><td>Full width of the column, framed. <code>--im-video-max</code> caps it.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-video-bleed</code></td><td>Edge to edge, corners squared — a band, not a card.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-video-wide</code> · <code>-square</code> · <code>-vertical</code></td><td>21:9, 1:1, and 9:16 capped at 24rem.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Video as a background</h2>
<p>A band of the page with a clip running behind it. The clip is <strong>decoration</strong>: muted, looping, <code>playsinline</code>, and never the only place anything is said. A gradient scrim — dark where the words are, clear above — keeps the copy readable whatever frame the clip is on.</p>
{{#example flush=true}}
<section class="im-videobg" style="--im-videobg-min: 22rem">
	<video class="im-videobg-media" src="{{demo.video}}" muted loop playsinline autoplay></video>
	<div class="im-videobg-body">
		<p class="im-caption" data-index="01">Sections</p>
		<h3 class="im-videobg-title">Forty kilometres, two hours, nobody in a hurry.</h3>
		<p class="im-videobg-text">The clip sits behind the words and never asks to be watched.</p>
		<div class="im-cluster"><a class="im-btn im-btn-primary" href="#video-as-a-background">Read it {{> "icons/arrow-right"}}</a><a class="im-btn im-btn-ink" href="#video-as-a-background">Watch</a></div>
	</div>
</section>
{{/example}}

<h2>Words blended into the clip</h2>
<p>Two ways, and both are <strong>one blend mode</strong> doing the work. There is no way to pour a <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> into the letters of a heading with <code>background-clip: text</code>, because a video is not a background image — so the sheet gets blended instead of the letters.</p>
{{#example flush=true}}
<section class="im-videobg im-videobg-clear" style="--im-videobg-min: 20rem">
	<video class="im-videobg-media" src="{{demo.video}}" muted loop playsinline autoplay></video>
	<div class="im-videobg-knockout"><h3>THE LONG WAY ROUND</h3></div>
</section>
{{/example}}
<p><strong>Knockout</strong> — <code>im-videobg-knockout</code> — is a sheet of white with black words on it, blended <code>screen</code>. Screen keeps white white and lets black through entirely, so the sheet stays solid and the <em>words</em> become windows onto the clip. Dark mode flips both: a black sheet, white words, blended <code>multiply</code>. Use <code>im-videobg-clear</code> with it, since the sheet is doing the scrim's job.</p>
{{#example flush=true}}
<section class="im-videobg im-videobg-clear im-videobg-center" style="--im-videobg-min: 18rem">
	<video class="im-videobg-media" src="{{demo.video}}" muted loop playsinline autoplay></video>
	<div class="im-videobg-body">
		<h3 class="im-videobg-title im-videobg-blend">Sorrento → Amalfi</h3>
	</div>
</section>
{{/example}}
<p><strong>Blend</strong> — <code>im-videobg-blend</code> — puts the words themselves on <code>difference</code>. They invert whatever passes under them, so they stay legible over a bright frame and a dark one with no scrim at all. It is the louder of the two: right for one line, wrong for a paragraph.</p>
<p>Both need something to blend <em>with</em>: the band sets <code>isolation: isolate</code>, so a blended child mixes with the clip behind it and not with the whole page underneath.</p>

<h2>Rules for a background clip</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Muted, looping, <code>playsinline</code>, and short.</strong> A background clip that cannot loop cleanly is a distraction with a seam in it.</li>
	<li><strong>Give it a poster.</strong> It is the first frame a reader sees and the only one they see on a slow connection.</li>
	<li><strong>Never put anything in it that is only in it.</strong> A reader with data saving on, reduced motion set, or a blocked autoplay sees the poster and nothing else.</li>
	<li><strong>Weigh it.</strong> A 12MB hero clip is the whole page's budget spent on the part nobody came for.</li>
</ul>
